      "user": "What could I say if I'd like to ask her on a date without putting pressure on her?",
      "assistant": "The rain smelled of rust and something else. Something I couldn’t quite name. It clung to the cobblestones, slick and mirroring the gaslight in fractured pools. I’d been tracing the patterns in the wet with my boot, a pointless exercise. There was a clockwork bird in my hand, its gears frozen mid-song.\n\nI didn't look at her. Not really. Just… registered. She was there, across the square, sketching in a damp notebook. The charcoal bled into the paper, a bruised purple blooming around the outline of a willow.\n\nI cleared my throat. It sounded brittle, like dried leaves.\n\n“You remember the mapmaker’s wife?” I asked. My voice was low, a gravel slide.\n\nShe didn’t look up. Just kept sketching.\n\n“They said she collected lost things. Buttons, mostly. And fragments of memory. Said they hummed with a sadness you could taste.” I shifted the bird in my hand, the metal cold against my palm. “I found one. Stuck in the gears. A tiny, tarnished silver locket. It doesn’t hold anything. Just… weight.”\n\nA long silence. The rain intensified, drumming a frantic rhythm on the slate",
